[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/1ppp1ppp/p1n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_4"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ You asked his bishop the big question. Now watch how he answers it, and keep counting who guards what in the centre. }
4. Ba4 { He does not take: the bishop slides back and still aims straight at your knight. Several of your pieces are still at home: which should wake up first? [%csl Ra4] } 4... Nf6 { Your last sleeping knight wakes up, and it pokes his centre pawn on the way past. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... b5 { You may chase the bishop straight away. Perfectly good, and it costs you a small amount of time. } 5. Bb3 Nf6 { The bishop tucks itself in and you develop anyway. We land in much the same kind of position. }) (4... Bc5 { A perfectly good square: the bishop comes out and looks at the squares near his king. } 5. c3 Nf6 { Your knight comes out one move later, and he has started propping up a pawn push. }) (4... d6 { Solid and slow. The pawn guards your centre pawn and shuts your dark bishop in behind it. } 5. O-O b5 { Playable, and he is very comfortable while your dark bishop stares at its own pawn. }) (4... Be7 { Safe, and a beat early: the bishop takes its square before the knight beside it has come out. } 5. O-O b5 { You are absolutely fine here, just very quiet, and that knight still has to come. }) 5. O-O { He castles and leaves his centre pawn loose. Grabbing it starts a wild fight, and there is a calm move here too. Can you find it? } 5... Be7 { Calm and correct. Your bishop guards the knight in front of it and clears the last square between your king and the corner. } (5... Nxe4 { You may take it, and it is every bit as good: this is the open Spanish, and it turns sharp in a hurry. } 6. d4 Be7 7. Re1 { A serious line with an awful lot to remember. Ours keeps the pawns level and the game quiet. }) (5... b5 { Chasing the bishop first is perfectly good too, and it has a snug square waiting for it. } 6. Bb3 Be7 7. Re1 { You reach our line with the moves in a different order, and his bishop has already stepped back. }) (5... d6 { Safe, slow, and a little sad for your dark bishop, whose diagonal now ends at your own pawn. } 6. Re1 b5 { Just as good in the end, though your dark bishop still waits at home behind its pawn. }) (5... Bc5 { An active square, and he gains time on the bishop by rolling his pawns at it. } 6. c3 O-O 7. d4 { The pawn kicks your bishop and grabs the centre, though you are still fine. }) 6. Re1 { His rook props the centre pawn up, so now taking your knight really would win a pawn. What now? [%csl Re5] } 6... b5 { Your pawn hits the bishop and blocks its road to your knight in the same move, so winning your centre pawn is off. [%cal Gb5a4] } (6... O-O { The natural move, and here it simply hands over your centre pawn. } 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. Nxe5 { His rook guards his king pawn now, so your queen has no double attack to win it back. The pawn is gone. }) (6... d6 { Solid, and it props the centre pawn up. Just as good as ours, and it asks him one question fewer. } 7. c3 O-O { A fine game for you. Shoving the bishop away first makes him decide sooner. }) (6... Bc5 { An active square for the bishop, and he gains time on it by building a pawn centre. } 7. c3 b5 { His pawns are ready to roll, and you have to shove the bishop away in any case. }) (6... Nd4 { A tempting jump into the middle, and he simply trades it off with a move to spare. } 7. Nxd4 exd4 8. c3 { His pawn pokes yours, his pieces come out faster, and he is slightly better. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the Scotch centre push"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/7h1Hmim8"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The same opening again, and you each have a pawn planted in the centre. This time watch his queen pawn. }
3. d4 { The Scotch. His queen pawn crashes in beside your pawn and offers a straight swap in the centre. What do you do about it? } 3... exd4 { You take it. A pawn in your pocket, and he has to spend a move fetching it back while you get on with your pieces. } (3... Nxd4 { The wrong piece takes. You swap off your best developed piece and his queen ends up in the centre for free. } 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. Qxd4 { His queen sits in the middle of the board, and not one of your pieces is out to bother her. }) (3... d6 { Too small a step. He shoves the pawn straight past and takes a big slice of the board for nothing. } 4. d5 Nb8 { Your knight has to trudge all the way home again while he stretches his legs. }) (3... d5 { Both centres crash together, and he takes first: his knight grabs your centre pawn. } 4. Nxe5 dxe4 5. Bb5 { The pawns are even, but his bishop has your knight nailed in front of your king. }) (3... Bd6 { The bishop parks right in front of your own queen pawn and locks it in for the rest of the opening. } 4. c3 exd4 5. cxd4 { He ends up with a perfect pair of centre pawns and your bishop is in your own pawn's way. }) (3... Bc5 { A natural square for the bishop, but his queen pawn already attacks it. } 4. dxc5 { His pawn takes your bishop, and you are a whole piece down. }) (3... Nf6 { A developing move, and it leaves your centre pawn to be taken. } 4. dxe5 Nxe4 5. Qd5 Nc5 { The pawns are even, but his queen chased your knight away and his pawn has pushed deep into your half. }) (3... Bb4+ { A check, but it does not help: your centre pawn is still attacked. } 4. c3 Bd6 { His pawn chased your bishop back, and now it stands in front of your queen pawn. He is better. }) 4. Nxd4 { He fetches his pawn back with the knight, and now nothing at all is guarding his centre pawn. Develop, and develop with menace. } 4... Nf6 { Out and poking: your knight asks his centre pawn some hard questions and brings your king one step nearer the corner. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... Bc5 { A natural square and perfectly playable. It does hand him the chance to gain time by shoving your bishop about. } 5. Nb3 Bb6 { Fine for you, but he chased the bishop and tidied a piece away in the very same move. }) (4... Bb4+ { A good check and a real line, just a shade less comfortable than ours. He blocks with a pawn and your bishop steps aside. } 5. c3 Bc5 { About level. He has a pawn on a useful square and your bishop has moved twice. }) (4... Qf6 { Your queen comes out early to poke at his knight, and he simply steps the knight away. } 5. Nb3 Qg6 { Your queen moves again to hit his king pawn, and he spent his move pulling a knight back. Nobody is ahead. }) (4... d5 { The centre break, and one move too early: you have nothing out to back it up. } 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. Bb5 { His pieces come out with threats attached and your knight is nailed in front of your king. }) 5. Nc3 { His last knight comes out, and it is the only thing guarding his centre pawn. What now? [%csl Rc3,Re4] } 5... Bb4 { Your bishop nails his knight to his own king. That knight cannot move a step, and his centre pawn has lost its only guard. [%cal Gb4e1,Gf6e4] } (5... Nxe4 { You grab the pawn, and he takes your knight: a real line, and you have to win the piece back. } 6. Nxe4 Qe7 7. Be2 Qxe4 { Your queen takes the knight back at once. You are a pawn up, but his pieces come out faster. }) (5... Bc5 { A natural square: your bishop and knight both hit his knight, and only his queen guards it. He has to look after it. } 6. Be3 Bb6 { He develops and backs the knight up, you tuck the bishop away, and you are a shade worse. }) (5... Be7 { Too quiet. He swaps knights and then queens, and his pieces come out faster than yours. } 6. Nxc6 dxc6 7. Qxd8+ Bxd8 { Queens are off, your bishop has gone back to your back row, and his pieces are the freer ones. }) (5... a6 { A slow little pawn move in a position where his pieces are flying out. } 6. Bc4 Bb4 { You play the bishop out anyway, a whole move behind where you could have been. }) 6. Nxc6 { He swaps your knight off, and two different pawns can take it back. They lead to two completely different games. [%csl Gb7,Gd7] } 6... bxc6 { The wing pawn takes, so your queen pawn stays home and blocks the road between the queens. He gets no queen swap. [%csl Gd7] } (6... dxc6 { Natural, and it opens the road between the two queens for a trade you really do not want. } 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Queens off, your king standing on her square, and castling gone for the rest of the game. }) (6... O-O { You tucked your king away and never took his knight back, and it now attacks your queen and your bishop at once. } 7. Nxd8 Rxd8 { His knight took your queen, and your rook got only a knight back. You are a queen down. }) (6... Nxe4 { You grab the free pawn and forget his knight on your knight's square: it attacks your queen. } 7. Nxd8 Kxd8 { Your king takes the knight back, but your queen is gone, and the game is lost. }) (6... Bxc3+ { Playable, and it hands him both bishops. It doubles his pawns, and here that suits him fine. } 7. bxc3 bxc6 { He has both bishops, and your knight is hitting his unguarded king pawn. It is about level. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the early queen raid"]
[Result "0-1"]
