[Role "exercise"]

{ You met him in the centre again, pawn for pawn. This time watch what he throws at you on move two, and do not panic. }
2. Qh5 { Out comes his queen on move two, too early, and she glares at your centre pawn, which has nobody guarding it. Answer her properly. } 2... Nc6 { Guard and develop in a single move. That is how you answer a bully: bring out a piece that does two jobs. [%csl Ge5] } (2... g6 { Never shoo his queen with that pawn. It does not guard your centre pawn, so she simply helps herself to it. } 3. Qxe5+ { Check, and a double attack: your king and your rook in the corner. [%cal Re5h8] } 3... Qe7 4. Qxh8 { Your queen stopped the check, and his queen took the rook in the corner. You are a rook and a pawn down. }) (2... Nf6 { It develops, and the pawn drops with check. Your pieces come out so fast that the game stays perfectly playable. } 3. Qxe5+ Be7 { A pawn down, but your pieces come out fast and chase his queen about. Ours guards the pawn instead. }) (2... d6 { This guards the pawn too, and it is every bit as good. It does shut your dark bishop in for a while. } 3. Bc4 g6 { A fine game. Our knight guards and develops at the same time, which is one job more. }) (2... Qe7 { Solid, and your queen really does guard the pawn. She also stands in her own bishop's doorway. } 3. Bc4 Nf6 { Playable, and a shade awkward: your bishop has to wait for the queen to move again. }) (2... Bd6 { It guards the pawn, and it stands on your own queen pawn's toes so that pawn cannot move. } 3. Bc4 Qe7 { Cramped, with your pieces treading on each other, and he is slightly the better placed. }) (2... Qf6 { Your queen guards the pawn and the weak square beside your king, but she takes your knight's best square. } 3. Nc3 Nc6 { Playable: your centre pawn has two guards, and your knight simply comes out on the other side. }) 3. Bc4 { Now his bishop joins his queen, and both of them stare at the red square beside your king. That is the four move mate. Stop it. [%csl Rf7] } 3... g6 { The little pawn shoos the queen away and shuts the door on the sore square beside your king at the same time. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it walks straight into mate. } 4. Qxf7# { His queen lands beside your king with the bishop guarding her, so nothing can take her. Scholar's mate. }) (3... d5 { It pokes his bishop, and his bishop simply takes the pawn. Your knight is the next thing on that diagonal. } 4. Bxd5 Qf6 5. Bxc6+ bxc6 { You took back, and he is a clean pawn up with your queen side pawns split. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending: your queen guards the sore square beside your king and nothing is hanging. } 4. Qd1 Nf6 { He gives up and sends his queen home. A perfectly good game, and your queen did the guarding. }) (3... Nh6 { That knight guards the sore square beside your king, and it is a miserable piece out on the edge. } 4. d3 g6 { The pawn shoos his queen away in the end, and now you are stuck with a knight out on the rim. }) (3... Qf6 { Your queen guards the red square too, but she stands on your knight's best square. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf6f7] } 4. Nc3 Bc5 { Playable, just clumsy: your queen is in your knight's way, and the pawn push was tidier. }) (3... d6 { It props up your centre pawn, but it does nothing about the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 4. Qxf7# { Checkmate. His queen lands beside your king with the bishop behind her. }) (3... Bc5 { A good square for the bishop, but only your king guards the red square, and that is not enough. [%csl Rf7] } 4. Qxf7# { Checkmate. Cover the square beside your king first, and develop afterwards. }) (3... Be7 { A quiet developing move, and it leaves the red square open. [%csl Rf7] } 4. Qxf7# { Checkmate. A quiet developing move does nothing when his queen and bishop both hit the square beside your king. }) 4. Qf3 { His queen slides across and aims at the red square once more, with the bishop still pointing there too. Stop the mate. [%csl Rf7] } 4... Nf6 { Your knight steps into the doorway and develops while it is there. His queen's big idea is dead. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, and it leaves the road to your king wide open. } 5. Qxf7# { Mate! The queen drops in beside your king with the bishop propping her up. The road had to be blocked. }) (4... Qe7 { Your queen guards the green square, and that is perfectly good defending too. [%csl Gf7] } 5. Ne2 Na5 { You are fine, and you chase his bishop into the bargain. The mate never had a chance. }) (4... Qf6 { Offering a swap of queens. Playable, but your queen stands on your knight's best square. } 5. Qd1 Bc5 { He sends his queen home rather than swap. You are fine, though your queen is still in your knight's way. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, and it parks your knight on the edge where it does the least work. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Perfectly playable, but blocking the queen's road with that knight was tidier. }) (4... f5 { It blocks the queen's road to your king, and it pulls the pawn away from in front of your own king. } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Still perfectly fine, and you have a draughty door to keep an eye on for a long time. }) (4... Nd4 { Your knight attacks his queen, and forgets the red square behind the attack. [%csl Rf7] } 5. Qxf7# { Checkmate. His queen did not need to run: she had a mate instead. }) (4... d6 { It props up your centre pawn and leaves his queen's road to your king wide open. [%csl Rf7] } 5. Qxf7# { Checkmate. His queen came straight down the column to the square beside your king. }) (4... Bc5 { A good square for the bishop, but his queen's road to the red square is still open. [%csl Rf7] } 5. Qxf7# { Checkmate. Block the queen's road first, and bring the bishop out afterwards. }) 5. Ne2 { His knight finally comes out. Two queen trips, and all he has to show for them is your pawn on a useful square. } 5... Bg7 { Your bishop fills the square your pawn left behind. Three pieces out, nothing hanging, and his raid has won him nothing. [%csl Gg7] } (5... d6 { Just as good, maybe even a shade more precise: it opens the road for your other bishop too. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { Your bishop comes out one move later, and your position is a picture of health. }) (5... h6 { A quiet little pawn move while your bishop is still waiting to come out. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { The bishop comes out in the end, and he used your quiet move to bring another piece into the game. }) (5... Na5 { Going after his bishop. Perfectly playable, and a little slow when a piece is still at home. } 6. d3 Bg7 { The bishop was the piece that mattered, and it comes out one move later. }) (5... a5 { A pawn step out on the wing with no plan behind it. Nothing breaks, and nothing improves either. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { You put the bishop on its square at last, one move later than you needed to. }) (5... Nd4 { Chasing the queen works only when nothing can take your knight, and here his knight can. } 6. Nxd4 exd4 7. O-O { One pair of knights is gone, your pawn in the middle stands alone, and he has castled. He is a little better. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: he plays the queen pawn"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/XaSSh7aB"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR_w_KQkq_-_0_1"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Now for his other big opening move. Today's new word is the pin, and he is going to try one on you before this game is out. }
1. d4 { He pushes the queen pawn instead. Same plan, other half of the centre: your queen pawn marches out to meet it. [%cal Yd7d5] } 1... d5 { Face to face, and neither pawn can get past the other. Nobody is handing over the centre today. } (1... Nf6 { Excellent too, and the most flexible move on the board. It leads to a whole world of openings of its own. } 2. c4 g6 { That is the Indian world, full of sharp ideas. Today we keep it simple and answer him pawn for pawn. }) (1... e6 { Playable, and it can even turn into our line. It does shut your light bishop in before it has ever been out. } 2. e4 d5 { And there you are in the French, arriving through the back door. }) (1... c6 { A waiting move, and a decent one. That little pawn would rather be propping up a pawn in the centre. } 2. c4 d5 { And there you are in the Slav, a fine opening where the little pawn props up your centre pawn. }) (1... d6 { Too small a step. He takes the rest of the centre with his king pawn and you have to fight your way in. } 2. e4 Nf6 { He has two fat pawns side by side and you are poking at them from underneath. }) 2. c4 { The queen's gambit: he offers a pawn beside his own to lure your centre pawn away from the middle. Prop yours up. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { Propped up. Your centre pawn is staying exactly where it is, and your dark bishop now has a door to walk through. } (2... dxc4 { The queen's gambit accepted, and it is perfectly sound. You will not be allowed to hold on to the pawn. } 3. e4 { Two big pawns take the centre for him, and he collects your extra pawn back at his leisure. }) (2... c6 { The Slav. A very good move: it props the centre pawn up and leaves your light bishop its diagonal. } 3. Nf3 Nf6 { Solid, serious stuff, and a whole opening of its own. Ours props the centre up with the king pawn. }) (2... Nc6 { This knight sits right in front of one of your pawns, and now that pawn cannot move at all. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. e3 { His centre is bigger than yours and your queen came out on move three. }) (2... c5 { Very sharp and very early. He takes in the centre and your queen has to come out and fetch the pawn back. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 { His pieces fly out and your queen has to watch for a knight jumping up to hit her. }) 3. Nc3 { He develops and leans on your centre pawn. Send out the knight that helps to guard it. [%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { Out it comes, and your centre pawn has a friend. Three of yours guard it now and two of his attack it. } (3... c6 { The Semi-Slav, every bit as good as ours and a whole opening of its own. The centre pawn gets one more guard. } 4. e3 Nf6 { You bring the knight out anyway, and he has built his little pawn wall in the meantime. }) (3... Bb4 { Just as good: your bishop leans on the knight that is leaning on your centre. It is a sharper road. } 4. e3 Nf6 { The same knight move by a rougher path, and the game turns messy much faster. }) (3... c5 { The sharp break, and every bit as good as ours. Your little pawn hits his centre at once. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { A real opening in its own right: your two pawns stand side by side, and his knight already leans on one. }) (3... a6 { A slow little move while the centre is still being argued over. Playable, just not very useful. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { He develops comfortably, and your little pawn move is fine but did not help much. }) (3... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop comes out first, and the knight follows. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 { Playable for you, and his bishop throws the first punch from outside his pawns. }) 4. Bg5 { The pin: his bishop hits your knight, and your queen stands right behind it, so moving the knight would lose her. Block the road. [%cal Rg5d8,Yf8e7] } 4... Be7 { Your bishop steps into the gap between your knight and your queen. The knight can move again and the pin is dead. [%csl Ge7] } (4... dxc4 { Grabbing the pawn here is also playable, and people play it on purpose. } 5. e3 c5 { A serious line, and a great deal more to remember than ours. }) (4... Bb4 { A pin against a pin: your bishop leans on the knight that holds his centre together. Sharp, and just as good. } 5. e3 c5 { Playable and fighting. Ours is the calm road, and a calm road is easier to steer. }) (4... c6 { Solid and a touch passive: it props the centre up and leaves the pinned knight to look after itself. } 5. e3 Nbd7 { Fine, and you end up bringing a piece over to guard that knight in any case. }) (4... Nbd7 { Also solid, and rather clever: your second knight guards the pinned one, so the pin costs you nothing. } 5. cxd5 exd5 { Playable and comfortable. We answer the pin by filling the gap with the bishop instead. }) 5. e3 { He builds a small, solid wall for his pieces to stand behind. Your king has waited long enough: send him to the corner. [%cal Ye8g8] } 5... O-O { Your king is tucked in the corner, your knight and bishop are out, the pin is answered and nothing hangs. A fine opening. [%csl Gg8] } (5... h6 { Asking the bishop the question first is very good indeed: he has to step back or swap it for your knight. } 6. Bh4 O-O { He keeps it, you castle, and you have gained a handy little pawn move for free. }) (5... Nbd7 { A good square and just as good a move, and your king is still standing about in the centre. } 6. Nf3 O-O { You castle one move later, and your king is just as safe. }) (5... b6 { Fine, and a little slow with your king still in the middle of the board. } 6. cxd5 Nxd5 { Playable, and he is a shade better with the freer pieces. }) (5... dxc4 { Also playable: you give up your centre pawn, and his bishop takes it while coming out. } 6. Bxc4 a6 { Level, though his bishop came out for free while taking back. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the London bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/w8eQu5SA"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq d6 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_w_KQkq_d6_0_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ You have met him in the centre, pawn for pawn. Now watch his dark bishop. }
2. Bf4 { The London. He lets that bishop out before his pawns can shut it in, and he plays it against almost anything. How do you answer? [%csl Rf4] } 2... Nf6 { Your knight comes out, guards the square beside his bishop and keeps every one of your options open. [%cal Gf6e4] } (2... c5 { A fine punch at his centre pawn, and every bit as good as ours. It does invite a lot of early swapping. } 3. e3 Nf6 { Also good. We bring a piece out first and keep the position simple to steer. }) (2... e6 { Playable, and it shuts your light bishop in a little earlier than it needs to be. } 3. e3 c5 { Fine chess. Developing the knight first leaves that bishop one more door. }) (2... Bf5 { Also fine: this bishop gets outside your own pawns before they can lock it in behind them. } 3. c4 e6 { A real line, and he pokes at your centre pawn straight away. }) (2... Nc6 { Natural, and it sits right in front of your own pawn, so that pawn cannot move at all. } 3. e3 Nf6 { He is comfortable, and one of your pawns is frozen for as long as the knight sits there. }) (2... e5 { A pawn into the centre, but his bishop is already looking at that square. } 3. Bxe5 { His bishop simply takes it, and you are a pawn down. }) 3. e3 { Up goes his little pyramid, with the bishop safely outside it. Your dark bishop is still shut in behind your own pawns. [%csl Rf8] } 3... e6 { Your pawn steps up and the road in front of your dark bishop opens, so it can go and look his in the eye. [%csl Gf8] } (3... c5 { That pawn punches straight at his centre, and it is every bit as good as ours. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 { Also fine. Going after his best bishop is our idea instead. }) (3... g6 { A different plan and quite playable: the bishop goes out to the wing instead. } 4. c3 Bg7 { Fine chess, but from there your bishop stares past his rather than meeting it. }) (3... Bf5 { The bishop comes out before the pawn, which is a real idea. Here he offers a swap of the two light ones. } 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. cxd3 { Just as good: the light bishops are swapped, and his rook can use the column his pawn left. [%cal Rc1c8] }) (3... Bg4 { Your bishop hits his queen at once. Playable, and just as good as ours. } 4. Qd3 { His queen steps aside and threatens a check that also hits the wing pawn your bishop stopped guarding. About level. }) 4. Nf3 { His knight comes out. That dark bishop is the piece he is proudest of, sitting outside his pawns. Where does your own dark bishop belong? [%csl Rf4,Gf8] } 4... Bd6 { Offer him the swap: his favourite bishop for the one of yours that was stuck at home. What a deal. [%cal Gd6f4] } (4... c5 { A fine move, hitting his centre at once, and it is worth exactly as much as ours. } 5. c3 Nc6 { Just as good. Trading his proudest piece off the board is our idea. }) (4... Be7 { Safe, and it lets his favourite bishop live a long and very happy life. } 5. c4 O-O { Perfectly playable, and he keeps the better of the two dark bishops. }) (4... a6 { A little waiting move. Nothing wrong with it, and nothing whatever gained. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { He brings another piece out in peace while you shuffle a pawn. }) (4... Bd7 { This bishop has nothing at all to do on that square, and it takes a square your other knight wanted. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { Playable, but the other bishop was the one with a job to do. }) 5. Bxd6 { He takes at once. Two different pieces of yours can take back, and they lead to two very different games. } 5... cxd6 { Your pawn takes, so your centre pawns stand one behind the other, and your rook can use the column next to your queen. [%csl Gd6,Gd5][%cal Gc7c2] } (5... Qxd6 { Also completely fine, and worth exactly as much as ours. Your queen takes a good central square. } 6. c4 dxc4 { A perfectly good game. You help yourself to the pawn he pushes, and he comes and fetches it back later. }) (5... Ne4 { You left his bishop standing on the square where it had just eaten yours. } 6. Ba3 { The bishop strolls away to safety and you are a whole piece down for nothing. }) (5... Nc6 { Developing is lovely, and his bishop is still sitting there with your bishop in its pocket. } 6. Ba3 { A whole bishop gone, and he did not even have to fight for it. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: check before you take"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/dD9n1jv3"]
[Role "exercise"]
